Delivery happens in `internal/webhook` (`internal/webhook/webhook.go`), entered through `webhook.PostAsync`, so the HTTP call is fire-and-forget and doesn't block the create response. `Post` marshals the payload as JSON, POSTs it through a `safeClient` (with SSRF-oriented URL checks in `internal/webhook/validate.go`), and treats any non-2xx status as an error.

Signing follows Standard Webhooks: when the hook has a signing secret, the key is resolved by `resolveSigningKey` (base64-decoding a `whsec_`-prefixed secret, otherwise using the raw bytes), then an HMAC-SHA256 is computed over `"<msgID>.<timestamp>."` concatenated with the JSON body. The result goes out base64-encoded across three headers, `webhook-id`, `webhook-timestamp`, and `webhook-signature` with the `v1,` prefix (lines 142–149). New secrets are minted by `GenerateSigningSecret` from 32 random bytes.

